Connect many constituents to a macroscopic material state (@physics-matter-state: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/foundations/Absolute-temperature-units-and-reference-levels.lingua#r_36PSQZ15KGX9RBTGGBV1G7Q06S") { r_0830CAE5F449015AF4585C7380
A rigid-body model tracks position, motion and external interactions while suppressing most internal detail. A thermal description asks additional questions: what material is present, how much of it there is, what state it occupies, and how its internal energy can change. Reuse [Choose a physical system and a question](Choose-a-physical-system-and-a-question.lingua#r_03633FE49B5013971613976F7D), [Read electronic states and orbital pictures as models of evidence](../../chemistry/core/Read-electronic-states-and-orbital-pictures-as-models-of-evidence.lingua#r_05EBFD205C1399B2027FBE417B) and [Explain bulk properties through interactions and collective states](../../chemistry/general-chemistry/states/Intermolecular-forces-and-collective-behavior.lingua#r_2QKWV1YDBB61H0GZN5A4GWRAYH). A collection of atoms is not characterized by its centre-of-mass motion alone, and a chemical bonding description does not by itself specify a macroscopic temperature or pressure.

Choose the system and boundary before naming transfers. A closed system contains a fixed collection or amount of matter; energy may cross its boundary as heat or work. An isolated model excludes both matter and energy transfer. A control-volume description allows matter to cross a selected boundary and needs the energy carried with it. A closed vessel being heated is therefore not isolated. A boundary may move while keeping the same matter inside. These distinctions follow the shared chemical/engineering accounts and the explicit definitions in [Introduction to Engineering Thermodynamics — Claire Yu Yan](../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/sources/Introduction-to-Engineering-Thermodynamics-Yan.lingua#r_6AX93SRMGE0XXJTQ1NW6X9QXTW), section 1.2.

For a homogeneous region, density is mass divided by volume; for a nonuniform material a local density description needs an appropriate averaging scale. Mechanical pressure describes compressive normal force per area in an isotropic fluid model. A solid can support different directional normal and shear stresses, so one scalar pressure does not describe every possible load state. Temperature is a different state quantity, developed next. Internal energy includes unresolved microscopic motion and interactions relative to the macroscopic motion being tracked; it is not identical to the kinetic energy of the centre of mass.

Equilibrium refers to the absence of the relevant macroscopic driving changes under the declared constraints. Thermal, mechanical, phase and chemical equilibrium are related but distinct conditions. A steady heated flow can keep its measured profiles constant while transporting energy and producing irreversible change. A local-equilibrium model assigns state quantities to small regions of a nonuniform system; this requires meaningful separation of microscopic relaxation and macroscopic observation scales. A fine numerical grid does not establish that physical separation.

An equation of state relates some state variables under a material model. For a fixed amount of a simple ideal gas, pV=nRT relates absolute pressure, volume and thermodynamic temperature. It neither specifies an arbitrary material’s internal energy nor predicts the rate at which equilibrium is approached. A gas undergoing a rapid uneven expansion may have no single equilibrium pressure throughout its interior, even when its final state can be described by that equation. The shared gas and caloric-model lessons will make these limits concrete.

**Read and compare:** [Introduction to Engineering Thermodynamics — Claire Yu Yan](../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/sources/Introduction-to-Engineering-Thermodynamics-Yan.lingua#r_6AX93SRMGE0XXJTQ1NW6X9QXTW), sections 1.2–1.4 and the opening properties discussion; [University Physics Volume 2 — OpenStax](../electromagnetism/sources/University-Physics-Volume-2.lingua#r_5JQ0CPQ0RNJNVH175Y9V305J5Q), sections 1.1 and 2.1. The former supplies the closed/isolated distinction used here. Preserve that distinction when encountering looser system wording in other introductions. Compare a sealed heated sample, a moving closed boundary and a flowing channel. For each, list the matter, state quantities, observed time scale and allowed transfers. State what the earlier rigid-body model would miss.

**Continue:** [Absolute temperature, units and reference levels](../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/foundations/Absolute-temperature-units-and-reference-levels.lingua#r_36PSQZ15KGX9RBTGGBV1G7Q06S).

